==Old English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈseːon/|lang=ang}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|sehwanan|lang=gem-pro}}.  Cognate with Old Frisian ''sīa'' (West Frisian ''[[sjen]]''), Old High German ''[[sehan]]'' (German ''[[sehen]]''), Old Saxon ''[[sehan]]'', Dutch ''[[zien]]'', Old Norse {{term|sjá|lang=non}} (Danish and Swedish {{term|se|lang=se}}, Icelandic {{term|sjá|lang=is}}), Gothic [[𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍈𐌰𐌽]].

====Verb====
{{ang-verb|head=sēon|type=strong|class=V}}

# to [[see]]

=====Conjugation=====
{{ang-conj|type=strong|V|sēo|siehst|siehþ|sēoþ|sēo|sēon|seah|sāwe, sǣġe|seah|sāwon, sǣgon|sāwe, sǣġe|sāwen, sǣġen|seoh|sēoþ|sēonde|(ġe)sewen, (ġe)seġen}}

=====Descendants=====
* English: [[see]]

===Etymology 2===
Akin to {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|sin|sīn|to be|lang=goh}}, {{etyl|la|-}} {{term|sum||to be|lang=la}}.

====Verb====
{{ang-verb|type=irreg|head=sēon}}

# To [[be]], used primarily in reference to God
#: '' Fæder ūser, þū sē '''eart''' on heofonum, '''sī''' þīn nama gehālgod '' &ndash; Our Father, who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name.

